Project: G-Doc enterprise with PostgreSQL backend and React/Vite frontend

Architecture overview:
- Frontend: React + TypeScript + Vite
- Backend: Node.js + Express + PostgreSQL
- Database: PostgreSQL schema and seed files in backend/db/
- Communication: frontend calls backend via /api/... endpoints

Important entry points:
- Root frontend: App.tsx, index.tsx, Vite config in vite.config.ts
- Backend entry: backend/src/server.js
- Backend config: backend/src/config.js
- Database layer: backend/src/db.js
- Repositories/API mapping: backend/src/repositories.js
- SQL schema + migrations: backend/db/schema.sql, backend/db/fix_tables.sql, backend/db/seed.sql

API contract:
- GET /api/health
- GET /api/:tableName
- POST /api/:tableName/upsert
- DELETE /api/:tableName/:id
- External portal endpoints under /api/external-portal/*
- Supported tables include: units, profiles, sectors, users, doc_types, classifications, cover_templates, interested, documents, processes, counter, contracts, access_levels, access_logs, portal_services

Environment:
- Root .env from .env.example for frontend
- backend/.env from backend/.env.example for backend
- Backend depends on DATABASE_URL and optional CORS_ORIGIN

Project notes for agent:
- Use backend/src files for server changes, not dist or built artifact files.
- Use MD/ files for documentation and context notes.
- Respect generic repository/tableName behavior: not all arbitrary tables are accepted.
- The app is designed to preserve existing IDs as TEXT in PostgreSQL.
- Do not treat the backend as a migration framework; schema changes are managed via SQL scripts.
